    "snippet": "Apr 30, 2019         Adding the 8087 co-processor chip to a system made floating point operations up to 100 times faster. The 8087’s architecture became part of later Intel processors, and the 8087’s instructions (although now obsolete) are still a part of today’s x86 desktop computers. I opened up an 8087 chip and took die photos with a microscope yielding the composite photo below. The labels show the main functional blocks, based on my reverse engineering. (Click here for a larger image.)   Summarize",
