    "title": "Microcode inside the Intel 8087 floating-point chip: register exchange",
    "snippet": "May 31, 2026         Researchers have reverse-engineered the microcode of the Intel 8087 floating-point processor, focusing on the FXCH instruction. The microcode uses 14 micro-instructions to perform register exchanges, revealing complex internal operations.The Intel 8087, introduced in 1980, used microcode stored in a ROM of 1,648 micro-instructions to implement its instruction set, including complex functions like square roots and trigonometric calculations. The group examined an actual chip via microscopy, mapping the microcode and its control logic.   Summarize",
